## 3b. VERDICT — the observational route is underpowered, and that is the finding

Two facts kill the observational identification, and both are worth stating explicitly in Ch3 rather than discovered by an examiner:

1. **Within-condition range is far too narrow.** The ordering effect that matters spans iota = 0 to 1. The variation actually available inside M1/M3/M4 has SD = 0.040, i.e. the data only ever wobbles about +/- 9 points along a 100-point axis. The pooled slope's CI ([-7.16, 3.68] in log-odds) is consistent with anything from a large harm to a large benefit. It is not evidence of no effect; it is an absence of evidence.

2. **Seeds do not re-randomise the layout.** Retrieval order is deterministic given the item, so re-running the same item under a different seed reproduces the same iota. Only 0 records show within-item layout variation, so the item-fixed-effects estimator has nothing to work with. This is a property of the harness, not of the data volume — more seeds would not help.

Residual entanglement between iota and item difficulty:

```
            iota_sd  r_iota_distance  r_iota_nshown                                           note
condition                                                                                         
b            0.0000              NaN            NaN  iota constant — no within-condition variation
b_reversed   0.0000              NaN            NaN  iota constant — no within-condition variation
m1           0.0290           0.1420         0.2644                                               
m1_chrono    0.0000              NaN            NaN  iota constant — no within-condition variation
m2           0.0000              NaN            NaN  iota constant — no within-condition variation
m3           0.0469          -0.0873         0.1789                                               
m3_chrono    0.0000              NaN            NaN  iota constant — no within-condition variation
m4           0.0409           0.1705         0.2104                                               
m4_chrono    0.0000              NaN            NaN  iota constant — no within-condition variation
```

The positive within-M1 slope in §2 is very likely this confound rather than a real benefit of scrambling: items whose evidence sits further back get scrambled more, and `evidence_distance` independently predicts success with a positive coefficient. Do not report it as a finding.

**Power required for the experimental arm.** A paired `b_reversed` vs `b` McNemar test, sized on the discordance rate actually observed in the M2-vs-B contrast (0.306), needs **n = 168 items** to detect the 11.9pp ordering effect at 80% power, alpha = 0.05. The Mode B generator already produces 219 distinct items, so **the arm is adequately powered on a single seed** and costs one run of one condition. That is the cheapest defensible answer available to this project.

## 4. Identification caveat — quote this alongside the numbers

iota is observational. It is determined by where the evidence happens to sit in the episode, so it correlates with `evidence_distance`, which independently predicts success. Every model conditions on evidence_distance and the fixed-effects model additionally holds the item constant, but residual confounding by unobserved item difficulty cannot be excluded. These are ordering-ADJUSTED contrasts, not causal architecture effects. The registered `b_reversed` arm and a `chronological_order: true` run of M1/M3/M4 remain the experimental test; this analysis pre-registers what that test should find.

